"Shows an uncertainty distribution through a density-colored strip combined with explicit interval and point summaries.\n\nUse when: A full inferential distribution and one or more interval levels should be readable together. Compact category rows are needed without a full violin silhouette.\n\nAvoid when: Color differences are too subtle for the audience or exact distribution comparisons dominate.\n\nVisual grammar: Position along the strip encodes possible parameter values. Fill intensity encodes density, while overlaid ticks and points encode named intervals and a central statistic.\n\nBuild: Obtain the posterior, bootstrap, or other stated uncertainty distribution. Map density to a perceptually ordered gradient and specify its normalization across rows. Overlay declared interval levels and a clearly identified median or mean.\n\nCheck: Check interval endpoints come from the same distribution as the gradient. Verify density color is not mislabeled as probability at an exact point. State whether density normalization is shared or independent across groups.\n\nPitfalls: Per-row normalization conceals differences in peak density and must be labeled. Use quantile dots or point intervals when readers cannot decode a continuous color gradient reliably.\n\nExample: Compare synthetic uncertain completion costs using density strips, medians, and labeled 50% and 95% credible intervals.\n\nAlternatives: Quantile dotplot, Error bar chart, Violin plot."
